## 3. Typography

### 3.1 Font Families

Use the following font system:

```css
:root {
  --font-ui: "Poppins", system-ui, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", sans-serif;
  --font-body: "Inter", system-ui, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", sans-serif;
  --font-mono: "JetBrains Mono", "Fira Code", "SFMono-Regular", Consolas, monospace;
}
```

### 3.2 Usage

Use `--font-ui` for:

* product wordmark
* navigation labels
* section headings
* buttons
* status labels
* compact UI text

Use `--font-body` for:

* chat messages
* research summaries
* paper-related text
* explanations
* notes
* output cards
* longer interface copy

Use `--font-mono` for:

* code blocks
* JSON previews
* tool input/output
* terminal-like areas
* LaTeX snippets
* formula-heavy content

### 3.3 Type Scale

```css
:root {
  --text-xs: 12px;
  --text-sm: 14px;
  --text-md: 16px;
  --text-lg: 18px;
  --text-xl: 22px;
  --text-2xl: 28px;
  --text-3xl: 40px;
  --text-hero: clamp(48px, 8vw, 96px);

  --line-height-tight: 1.12;
  --line-height-normal: 1.5;
  --line-height-reading: 1.65;
}
```

Typography rules:

* Hero or large title text should feel bold, calm, and spacious.
* Long research text should use `line-height: 1.6–1.7`.
* UI text should be compact but not cramped.
* Avoid overly decorative fonts.
* Avoid all-caps body copy.
* Use letter spacing only for small labels and eyebrow text.

Example label style:

```css
.ui-label {
  font-family: var(--font-ui);
  font-size: var(--text-xs);
  letter-spacing: 0.12em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--text-muted);
}
```

